10:36:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Wind is an important driver of storm surge, but it is not the only driver, and it does not determine rainfall the same way it determines wind damage. - **Storm surge:** Wind pushes water toward shore, but the resulting water level also depends on the storm’s *size*, track and angle of approach, forward speed, central pressure, coastline shape, shallow-water bathymetry, tides, and waves. A large, slower storm with lower peak winds can sometimes create more surge than a compact stronger one. - **Heavy rain:** Rainfall is mainly controlled by moisture supply, storm motion, atmospheric steering patterns, and terrain. A hurricane that stalls can produce extreme flooding even after its winds weaken greatly. So maximum sustained wind speed captures one major hazard and is related to others, but it cannot calculate them. That is why forecasters issue separate surge and rainfall forecasts rather than deriving them directly from the category.
